<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Evliya Çelebi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1611–c. 1684</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Evliya Çelebi was an Ottoman traveller and writer from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> who spent some forty years journeying through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and neighbouring lands. His ten-volume Seyahatname, or 'Book of Travels', describes cities, monuments, customs and languages from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, including a vivid account of the court of the Kurdish emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bitlis">Bitlis</a> in 1655. It is a major source for the 17th-century Ottoman world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/evliya-celebi.jpg" alt="Evliya Çelebi" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Globetrotter19 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Evliya_Celebi_by_Piros_Rost%C3%A1s_Bea_(2014)_in_Eger,_2016_Hungary.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
